      "summary": "Edit a resolver excerpt and inspect the actual candidate sequence against supplied DNS snapshots.",
      "objective": "Keep status on the supplied branch.remote.test service instead of the bare-name shadow. Resolve portal.remote.test as that given name before trying a suffixed shadow. Preserve (portal.remote.test.) as an absolute lookup. Keep (audit.remote.test.) unresolved even though the same name without its final dot can find a supplied suffixed record. Retain the branch.remote.test search suffix across both DNS snapshots.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 25,
      "prerequisites": [
        "lpic1-dns-search-suffixes"
      ],
      "steps": [
        "Inspect the supplied DNS records, edit the virtual search settings and verify every required lookup."
      ],
      "guide": [],
      "hints": [
        "Compare the actual relative and final-dot candidate lists before changing DNS data. Only this search excerpt is editable.",
        "ndots0 can break the short service even when the remote query looks right. A larger threshold can prefer a suffixed shadow.",
        "Keep the branch suffix. A supported threshold of1 or2 protects this task’s short and multi-dot remote names."
      ],
      "explanation": "The branch suffix plus ndots1 or2 tries the short status service under branch first and the multi-dot portal name as given first. A final dot bypasses all suffixes, including after a miss. The relative audit spelling may search after its initial miss; its absolute spelling remains unresolved. This is finite positive/missing-reply reasoning, not an OS resolver or packet trace.",
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/",
        "https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/resolv.conf.5.html",
        "https://sourceware.org/glibc/manual/latest/html_node/index.html"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Pure in-process search/domain/options-ndots excerpt only. DNS snapshots provide one IPv4 A answer or a definite missing name; no actual queries. The supplied hostname, not the desktop, determines the default domain. At most16KiB/128lines, three suffixes/four candidate names, ordinary ASCII labels and253-byte expanded names, ndots0–15, explicit root suffix, final-dot absolute names and first-positive stopping. Whole-line comments only. No nameserver routing, other options, unavailable/retry sequencing, HOSTALIASES/environment/other resolver flags, cache, CNAME, IPv6/multiple answers, DNSSEC, packets, timing, files, sockets or OS resolver calls. This is not actual glibc conformance or full109.4 coverage.",
